From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 00:16:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: offload PGP crypto ops to a web worker via the JS
 bridge
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On wasm, PGP operations (keygen, encrypt, decrypt) ran on the main
browser event loop through the shim's spawn_blocking, janking the UI
for seconds. The tokio facade now exposes tokio::offload — a JS-side
handler (op: string, payload: Uint8Array) -> Promise<Uint8Array>
backed by a worker pool running this same crate's wasm build — and the
four crypto call sites take a cfg(wasm32)-gated early return through it
when a handler is registered, falling back to the unchanged inline code
otherwise. Native builds are untouched.

Op surface (src/offload_ops.rs, compiled on all targets; both the
call-site encoders and the worker-side handle_op dispatcher live in one
module so the wire format cannot diverge; fields are [u32 LE len][bytes]
after 1-2 raw flag/meta bytes, keys travel in core's sqlite
serialization, DcKey::to_bytes/from_slice):

- keygen        addr -> secret key           (key.rs generate_keypair)
- pk_encrypt    seckey, plain, pubkeys*      (pgp.rs pk_encrypt; the
                closure body is extracted into pk_encrypt_sync so both
                the native spawn_blocking path and the worker share it)
- symm_encrypt  secret, plain, [sign key]    (e2ee.rs encrypt_symmetrically)
- decrypt_keys  ctext, seckeys*              (decrypt.rs, asymmetric arm)
- decrypt_psk   session key, ctext           (decrypt.rs, symmetric arm;
                the session key is looked up in sqlite main-side as
                before, only the packet decryption is offloaded)

Decryption replies: rPGP's Message is a streaming reader with no
serializer, so the worker re-encodes the decrypted message at the
packet level — read the data to the end (finalizing the running
signature hashes), rebuild the literal packet from the data (keeping
the file name; the literal header is not covered by OpenPGP
signatures), and write one-pass-signature/signature packets around it
in their original structure. Main-side Message::from_bytes + verify
then sees identical hashes, so signature validation still happens in
the trusted main instance. The encrypted-message side cannot be
serialized either, so the call sites re-extract the raw armored body
from the MIME part it was originally parsed from.

Secret keys and session keys do cross postMessage to the worker; both
ends are same-origin contexts of the same app that can already read
the key from sqlite, so this widens no trust boundary.

Round-trip tests (offload_ops::tests) cover keygen -> pk_encrypt ->
decrypt_keys for SEIPD v1+v2 and symm_encrypt -> decrypt_psk, asserting
content equality and that signatures verify after re-encoding.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Since v2.64.0 upstream queues mail before encryption and encrypts
synchronously in mimefactory::render_queued_mail() (EncryptHelper is
gone, pgp::pk_encrypt() is sync and its signing key optional). The
offloaded encrypt paths therefore moved:
offload_ops::pk_encrypt_offloaded()/symm_encrypt_offloaded() wrap
upstream's unchanged pgp functions, render_queued_mail() became async to
await them, the pk_encrypt payload carries the signing key only when
there is one (flag bit 2), and the worker calls upstream's pk_encrypt()
directly instead of a copied pk_encrypt_sync(). keyupdate_tests follows
the tuple returned by get_encrypted_pgp_message_boxed().
